---
/**
 * Copyright and DMCA notice-and-takedown page (Task 200). The notice and counter-notice lists follow the elements
 * named in 17 U.S.C. § 512(c)(3) and § 512(g)(3). The safe harbour also needs a designated agent registered with the
 * U.S. Copyright Office: until Christian registers one, the agent's details are <LegalPlaceholder> marks and the
 * production build is blocked. Not legal advice; attorney review pending.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LegalPlaceholder from '../components/LegalPlaceholder.astro';
import LegalApproval from '../components/LegalApproval.astro';
import { isLegalPageApproved } from '../config/legalapproval';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
---

<Layout title="copyright" description="Who owns what on Cueory, and how to report a copyright problem." noindex={!isLegalPageApproved("copyright")}>
  <Prose>
    <h1>copyright and takedown requests</h1>
    <LegalApproval page="copyright" />

    <h2>What belongs to whom</h2>
    <p>
      The lessons, questions, cards, puzzles, design, name and logo of {site.name} belong to{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="legal entity name, the same as on the terms page" /> or to the people who licensed them
      to us. Our source code is public to view on GitHub, but it is not open source. GitHub's own terms of service give
      other GitHub users the right to view, and to fork within GitHub, anything in a public repository; beyond that,
      the code may not be copied, changed or used elsewhere without our written permission.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="with a lawyer: confirm the GitHub terms wording (Terms of Service, section D, licence grant to other users), and decide whether anything in the public repository is too proprietary to stay there" /> Material from other creators, such as the open textbook we quote, the 3D anatomy models and
      the fonts, keeps its own licence; we credit each one on the <a href="/licenses">licences page</a> and beside the
      material itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Using our content</h2>
    <p>
      You may use {site.name} for your own personal, non-commercial study. Please don't copy, scrape, re-publish or
      re-sell Cueory-owned material (our text, layout and branding), and don't hit our servers with automated access.
      Material that carries a CC BY 4.0 credit, such as the adapted textbook text and the anatomy models listed on the{' '}
      <a href="/licenses">licences page</a>, stays reusable under its own licence with attribution; we add no
      restriction to it. If you want to use something of ours beyond personal study, email us and ask.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="with a lawyer: confirm which adaptations Cueory owns outright and which remain CC BY 4.0 derivatives" />
    
    </p>

    <h2>No public posting</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name} has no place where visitors publish content to each other. The only things a student can send us are
      private reports and comments through the "Flag or comment" button, a display name and an email address; none of
      them is shown to other visitors. Everything you read on the site was put there by us, so a complaint about it is a
      complaint about our own material, and we handle it the same way.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting a copyright problem</h2>
    <p>
      We respect other people's rights. If you believe material on {site.name} infringes a copyright you own or
      represent, email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a> with "copyright" in the subject.
      We will read it and review the material. We make no claim here that the safe harbour of 17 U.S.C. § 512 applies to
      {site.name}, and we have not registered a designated agent with the U.S. Copyright Office; if that changes, this
      page will say so and list the agent.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="with a lawyer: decide whether 17 U.S.C. § 512 applies to a site with no user-posted content and whether to register a designated agent anyway (Copyright Office fee $6 per designation; renew every 3 years; the registration needs the provider's physical street address, the agent's address may be a P.O. box; the public details here must match the registration exactly). If registered, replace this paragraph with the agent's name, email, phone number and mailing address." />
    </p>
    <p>A complete notice helps us act, and under the law it includes:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>your physical or electronic signature, or that of a person authorised to act for the copyright owner;</li>
      <li>which copyrighted work you say has been infringed (or a representative list, if there are several);</li>
      <li>where the material is on {site.name}, with enough detail, such as the page address, for us to find it;</li>
      <li>your name, mailing address, telephone number and email address;</li>
      <li>a statement that you believe in good faith that the use is not authorised by the copyright owner, its agent or the law;</li>
      <li>a statement, made under penalty of perjury, that the information in the notice is accurate and that you are the owner or authorised to act for the owner.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Knowingly misrepresenting that material is infringing can make you liable for damages. If you are not sure whether
      a use is infringing, consider getting legal advice before you send a notice.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do with a notice</h2>
    <p>
      We review every notice we receive. Where a complaint about our own material is well founded, we fix or remove it.
      If material a user provided is involved, we remove or disable it while we review and tell that user. We keep the
      notice with our records.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="confirm the intake practice (who reads the mailbox, where notices are logged, target response time) before this page promises any timing" />
    </p>

    <h2>If your material was removed: counter-notice</h2>
    <p>
      If something you provided was removed and you believe it was removed by mistake or misidentification, you may
      send a written counter-notice to the same email address. It must include:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>your physical or electronic signature;</li>
      <li>which material was removed, and where it appeared before it was removed;</li>
      <li>a statement, made under penalty of perjury, that you believe in good faith the material was removed by mistake or misidentification;</li>
      <li>
        your name, address and telephone number, and a statement that you consent to the jurisdiction of the federal
        district court for your address (or, if you are outside the United States, for any district in which we may be
        found), and that you will accept service of process from the person who sent the original notice or their agent.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Note that a counter-notice is forwarded in full, with your contact details, to the person who sent the original
      notice; our <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a> says how notices are kept. When we receive a valid counter-notice
      we send a copy to the person who sent the original notice. Unless they
      tell us within ten business days that they have filed a court action to stop the infringement, we may restore the
      material between ten and fourteen business days after we receive your counter-notice.
    </p>

    <h2>Repeat infringers</h2>
    <p>
      We may close the account of anyone who infringes copyright through the site, and we may remove any material at
      our discretion, whether or not a notice has been sent.{' '}
      <LegalPlaceholder what="with a lawyer: a formal repeat-infringer policy (strikes, records, termination) is one of the conditions of the § 512 safe harbour; adopt one here only if you pursue that safe harbour" />
    </p>

    <h2>Trademarks</h2>
    <p>
      "cueory" and the cueory logo are our marks. Exam, school and publisher names on the site belong to their owners
      and are used only to describe what a lesson is about.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      For anything other than a formal notice, email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
